<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!--
  X500 + Velodyne VLP-16. First step of the migration off iris_vlp16_cam: frame and lidar only.
  Full payload: VLP-16 on a mast, TFmini and A8 mini camera under the centre plate.
  Every mount height here is derived from measured mesh geometry; see each block.

  LIDAR HEIGHT IS SET BY THE PROPS, NOT BY TASTE. The full derivation, from both the prop
  collision discs and the prop visual meshes, is on the velodyne_link block below. Short form:
  the -15 deg ring must clear the outermost blade, which needs z > 0.1376; it is at 0.20.
  (That floor was 0.1668 before the 2026-09-06 airframe shrink. Smaller props pull the tip
  inward, so the ring clears sooner and the mount height did not need to move.)
  The iris got away with 0.12 only because its rotors sat much lower (z = 0.023).
  Anything that moves the rotors, lengthens the props, or lowers this mount must redo that sum.

  FAST-LIO's mapping/extrinsic_T must match this height. It is NOT updated automatically:
  see nidar/lidar/extrinsic_T in mission_config.yaml.
-->
<sdf version="1.6">
  <model name="x500_vlp16">
    <include>
      <uri>model://x500</uri>
    </include>

    <link name="velodyne_link">
      <!-- HEIGHT IS SET BY THE PROPS. Measured from the real x500 geometry, not guessed.
           UPDATED 2026-09-06 for the shrunk airframe (hub +-0.120, prop 0.200 m diameter):
             prop COLLISION disc r = 0.100, hub at r = hypot(.120,.120) = 0.16971,
                                 so the disc sweeps out to r = 0.2697 at z = 0.0625
             prop VISUAL  mesh   scaled 0.716253, outer tip now r = 0.2546, z top 0.0694
           The lowest ring leaves at -15 deg, so at radius r it has fallen r*tan(15) = 0.26795*r.
           Clearing the outermost blade needs
                 collision:  z > 0.0625 + 0.2697*0.26795 = 0.1348
                 visual:     z > 0.0694 + 0.2546*0.26795 = 0.1376   <- governs
           At z = 0.20 that is 62 mm of clear air over the blade, up from 33 mm before the
           shrink: smaller props move the tip inward, so the ring clears sooner. The mount
           height did NOT have to change. No scan line is chopped anywhere in the annulus.
           The previous 0.0889 (set by hand in the placement rig) crossed the prop plane at
           r = 0.108 m, right at the inner edge of the blade sweep, so the lower rings were cut
           across nearly the whole annulus. Restored to 0.20.
           x and y are zeroed: the rig left 0.7 mm / 0.1 mm of drag noise there, and a spinning
           lidar wants to be on the roll/pitch axis, not 0.7 mm off it.
           DO NOT EDIT THIS POSE BY HAND. It is generated from nidar.lidar.mount_height in
           mission_config.yaml, together with the mast length below and FAST-LIO's
           mapping/extrinsic_T, which must equal it exactly. Change the height there and run
               rosrun nidar_mission apply_mission_config.py
           which also refuses any height that puts the lowest ring back into the props. -->
      <pose>0 0 0.2 0 0 0</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.23</mass>
        <!-- Solid cylinder, r = 0.025, l = 0.0717, m = 0.23:
             ixx = iyy = m(3r^2 + l^2)/12 = 0.0001344,  izz = m r^2/2 = 0.00007188.
             Recomputed when the radius was reduced from 0.0516; leaving the old 0.000908 would
             have described a body twice this wide.

             MASS REDUCED 0.83 -> 0.23 kg on 2026-09-06 together with the airframe shrink. 0.83
             is a real VLP-16; 0.23 represents a compact solid-state unit of the Livox Mid-360
             class, which is what a 0.54 m airframe would actually carry. The inertia was
             rescaled by the same 0.2771 factor; do NOT change the mass without it, or you
             describe a 0.23 kg object that spins like an 0.83 kg one.
             Total vehicle mass 3.0043 -> 2.4043 kg, so T/W rises 1.90 -> 2.37 and
             MPC_THR_HOVER must drop to 0.55 (see the airframe file). -->
        <inertia>
          <ixx>0.0001344</ixx>
          <iyy>0.0001344</iyy>
          <izz>0.00007188</izz>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <visual name="velodyne_visual">
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.025</radius>
            <length>0.0717</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <name>Gazebo/DarkGrey</name>
            <uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <!-- Standoff mast: the support the lidar actually stands on. Prop clearance forces the
           sensor up to 0.20 while the centre plate top is at z = 0.032, so without this the
           lidar floats 0.13 m over the airframe with nothing holding it up.
           GENERATED, like the pose above: it runs from the plate top to the bottom of the
           sensor body, so raising mount_height lengthens the mast to match.
               length = mount_height - 0.0717/2 - 0.032
               pose z = 0.032 + length/2 - mount_height     (velodyne_link's frame)
           Visual only, on purpose. Mass and collision here would move the CoM and add contact
           geometry overlapping base_link for no gain: at r = 0.008 the mast is inside the
           sensor's own min_range, so the lidar can never see it either way. -->
      <visual name="velodyne_mast_visual">
        <pose>0 0 -0.101925 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry>
          <cylinder>
            <radius>0.008</radius>
            <length>0.13215</length>
          </cylinder>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <name>Gazebo/Grey</name>
            <uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <sensor type="ray" name="velodyne-VLP16">
        <pose>0 0 0 0 0 0</pose>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <update_rate>10</update_rate>
        <ray>
          <scan>
            <horizontal>
              <samples>1800</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-3.14159265359</min_angle>
              <max_angle>3.14159265359</max_angle>
            </horizontal>
            <vertical>
              <samples>16</samples>
              <resolution>1</resolution>
              <min_angle>-0.261799</min_angle>
              <max_angle>0.261799</max_angle>
            </vertical>
          </scan>
          <range>
            <min>0.05</min>
            <max>30</max>
            <resolution>0.001</resolution>
          </range>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.008</stddev>
          </noise>
        </ray>
        
        <plugin name="gazebo_ros_laser_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_velodyne_laser.so">
          <topicName>/velodyne_points</topicName>
          <frameName>velodyne_link</frameName>
          <min_range>0.05</min_range>
          <max_range>30</max_range>
          <gaussianNoise>0.008</gaussianNoise>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <joint name="velodyne_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>x500::base_link</parent>
      <child>velodyne_link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- TFmini Plus downward rangefinder, on the underside of the X500 centre plate.

         PLACEMENT, derived from geometry rather than guessed:
           * The mesh's lens pair is on its -X face (two identical r = 8.4 mm circles), so the
             model.sdf visual is rotated Ry(-90 deg) to bring that face down in line with the
             ray. The face that then points UP, and therefore mates with the airframe, is +X
             (the connector/back face).
           * X500 base_link_collision_0 is a 0.05 m box at z = +0.007, so the centre plate's
             UNDERSIDE is z = -0.018.
           * Rotated device height is 0.025 m, so seating its top flush against that plate puts
             the link at -0.018 - 0.0125 = -0.0305, and the lens face at -0.043.

         PARKED CLEARANCE, which is what killed this on the iris: the skid rails sit at
         z = -0.2195, so on the ground the lens is 0.2195 - 0.043 = 0.1765 m up. That is 76 mm
         clear of the TFmini's own 0.10 m minimum range, versus the 0.0584 m (BELOW the floor)
         the iris build produced, where FAST-LIO rejected every sample, lost its Z pin and the
         vehicle diverged. The X500's taller stance is what buys that margin.

         NAMING IS LOAD-BEARING on both sides. gazebo_mavlink_interface scans model joints for
         .*(lidar|sf10a)(.*), strips "_joint" and subscribes to ~/<model>/link/<that name>,
         while gazebo_lidar_plugin publishes from the scoped link name. "tfmini_lidar_joint"
         paired with model "tfmini_lidar" is what makes those meet. Rename either and PX4
         silently receives no DISTANCE_SENSOR while ROS /tfmini/range keeps working, leaving
         EKF2_HGT_REF=2 with no height source at all. -->
    <include>
      <uri>model://tfmini_lidar</uri>
      <!-- Placed in the rig on 2026-09-06. roll = -1.5369 is the 90 deg that turns the
           lens pair to face the ground; see the face survey in tfmini_lidar/model.sdf. -->
      <pose>-0.0328 -0.0005 -0.0573 -1.5369 -0.0046 0.0061</pose>
    </include>

    <joint name="tfmini_lidar_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>x500::base_link</parent>
      <child>tfmini_lidar::link</child>
    </joint>

    <!-- SIYI A8 mini, on the underside of the X500 centre plate.

         WHICH FACE MATES, determined from the mesh rather than assumed. Face survey of
         siyi_a8_mini.stl (51176 tris) measuring flat area per bbox face:
             -Z  0.00222 m2  (70% of that face)   <- much the flattest: the MOUNTING PLATE
             +Z  0.00067 m2  (21%)
             -X  0.00065 m2  (13%)   +X 0.00043 (8%)   -Y 0.00051 (12%)   +Y 0.00016 (4%)
         Curved geometry is 21.6% of total area, centred at z = +0.020 in the authored pose:
         that is the gimbal ball sitting ABOVE the plate. So as authored the part stands plate
         DOWN, ball up, the way it would sit on a bench.
         Hanging it under an airframe therefore needs it INVERTED: roll = pi puts the plate on
         top (z = +0.0113) to meet the airframe and drops the ball below.

         PLACEMENT:
           X500 centre-plate underside is z = -0.018, so link z = -0.018 - 0.0113 = -0.0293
           and the body hangs to z = -0.1020, still 0.1175 m above the skid rails at -0.2195.
           x = 0.08 puts it forward of the TFmini (which spans x -0.0175..+0.0175), leaving a
           0.0381 m gap, and well inside the 0.177 m half-width of the plate.

         This is the placement that repeatedly went wrong on the iris. There the +Z face was
         assumed to be the mount, which buried the body in the fuselage; and with no landing
         gear the camera became the touchdown point, so the vehicle sat nose-down on it at
         ~12 deg and PX4 refused to arm. Here the skids carry the vehicle and the camera hangs
         free between them. -->
    <link name="camera_link">
      <pose>0.0231 0.0043 -0.0518 3.1332 -0.0015 -3.0737</pose>
      <inertial>
        <mass>0.095</mass>
        <inertia>
          <ixx>6.5e-05</ixx>
          <ixy>0</ixy>
          <ixz>0</ixz>
          <iyy>6.5e-05</iyy>
          <iyz>0</iyz>
          <izz>4.8e-05</izz>
        </inertia>
      </inertial>

      <collision name="camera_collision">
        <geometry>
          <box>
            <size>0.052 0.061 0.084</size>
          </box>
        </geometry>
      </collision>

      <visual name="camera_visual">
        <geometry>
          <mesh>
            <uri>model://iris_vlp16_cam/meshes/siyi_a8_mini.stl</uri>
            <scale>1 1 1</scale>
          </mesh>
        </geometry>
        <material>
          <script>
            <name>Gazebo/DarkGrey</name>
            <uri>file://media/materials/scripts/gazebo.material</uri>
          </script>
        </material>
      </visual>

      <sensor name="camera" type="camera">
        <!-- WHERE THE CAMERA ACTUALLY LOOKS. The mesh orientation is cosmetic; this pose
             decides the optical axis, and the Gazebo GUI cannot show it, so it has to be
             solved rather than eyeballed.
             Two bugs were fixed here on 2026-09-06:
               1. the old value carried roll=pi on TOP of the link's own roll=pi. The two
                  cancel, and conjugating Ry by Rx(pi) flips the pitch sign, so the camera
                  looked 15 deg UP instead of down.
               2. the link pose set in the rig carries yaw = -3.0737 as well as roll = pi,
                  which together are a 180 deg flip about Y, so the camera also faced
                  BACKWARDS (azimuth -176 deg).
             Solved against the link pose actually in use, by requiring the world optical axis
             to be (cos15, 0, -sin15):
                 R_sensor . x_hat = R_link^-1 . (0.9659, 0, -0.2588)
             Verified with the real SDF composition Rz(yaw).Ry(pitch).Rx(roll):
                 world axis (+0.9659, +0.0000, -0.2588) = elevation -15.0 deg, azimuth 0.0 deg.
             RE-SOLVE THIS IF THE LINK POSE CHANGES: the two are coupled, and a link rotation
             that looks harmless in the GUI silently re-aims the camera. -->
        <pose>0 0 0 0 -0.259724 -3.071500</pose>
        <always_on>1</always_on>
        <update_rate>15</update_rate>
        <visualize>false</visualize>
        <camera name="a8_mini">
          <horizontal_fov>1.4137</horizontal_fov>
          <image>
            <width>640</width>
            <height>480</height>
            <format>R8G8B8</format>
          </image>
          <clip>
            <near>0.05</near>
            <far>100</far>
          </clip>
          <noise>
            <type>gaussian</type>
            <mean>0.0</mean>
            <stddev>0.007</stddev>
          </noise>
        </camera>
        <plugin name="camera_controller" filename="libgazebo_ros_camera.so">
          <alwaysOn>true</alwaysOn>
          <updateRate>15</updateRate>
          <cameraName>camera</cameraName>
          <imageTopicName>image_raw</imageTopicName>
          <cameraInfoTopicName>camera_info</cameraInfoTopicName>
          <frameName>camera_link</frameName>
          <hackBaseline>0.0</hackBaseline>
          <distortionK1>0.0</distortionK1>
          <distortionK2>0.0</distortionK2>
          <distortionK3>0.0</distortionK3>
          <distortionT1>0.0</distortionT1>
          <distortionT2>0.0</distortionT2>
        </plugin>
      </sensor>
    </link>

    <joint name="camera_joint" type="fixed">
      <parent>x500::base_link</parent>
      <child>camera_link</child>
    </joint>
  </model>
</sdf>
